EXT. FLOODED RICE PADDY - DAWN

Mist hangs low over water the color of tin. A broken irrigation sluice stands like a gallows in the middle distance.

Four soldiers crouch behind a mud dike, helmets slick with rain.

SERGEANT WALTER "BUCK" DOYLE (40s, square jaw, a face carved for recruiting posters) scans the treeline through field glasses.

CORPORAL RAFAEL ORTEGA (20s, radio on his back, quick to smile) fiddles with a dial.

PRIVATE EZRA FINCH (19, freckled, rifle held too tightly) shivers beside him.

LIEUTENANT HIROSHI TANAKA (30s, Nisei interpreter, spectacles taped at the hinge) kneels apart, a map folded in his gloved hands.

DOYLE
Quiet, all of you.

ORTEGA
I'm just saying, Sarge, the set's dead. Nothing but static.

FINCH
Maybe they're jamming it.

TANAKA
They aren't. The transmitter's been underwater since the rains.

DOYLE
I said quiet.

FINCH
Sarge, I think I saw something move.

ORTEGA
Where?

TANAKA
Left of the sluice. Don't point.

DOYLE
Finch, stay down. Ortega, forget the radio. Lieutenant, how far to the ridge?

TANAKA
Two kilometers across open water.

ORTEGA
Open water. Swell.

FINCH
I can't swim so good.

DOYLE
You can wade.

Across the paddy, a rooster crows. Everyone freezes. Then, from the treeline, a woman's voice calls out in a tongue none of them knows.

TANAKA
(low)
She says to come in. She says it is safe.

DOYLE
Do you believe her?

TANAKA
No.

ORTEGA
Then why are you smiling?

TANAKA
Because she said it twice.

A mortar whistles. All four dive into the mud as the sluice erupts in white water.

DOYLE
Move! Move!

They scramble along the dike, bent double, Finch last, dragging his rifle through the muck.
